2. Heckscher–Ohlin and Stolper–Samuelson Theorems
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: I | Topic: Heckscher–Ohlin, Stolper–Samuelson theorem
Introduction
Ricardo explained trade through differences in labour productivity but not why those differences exist. Eli Heckscher (1919) and Bertil Ohlin (1933) located the source in differing factor endowments. The Stolper–Samuelson theorem (1941) then traces how trade changes income distribution.
Assumptions of the H-O model
Two countries, two goods, two factors (capital and labour); identical technology and tastes; constant returns to scale; perfect competition; factors mobile within, immobile between countries; goods differ in factor intensity with no factor-intensity reversal.
The H-O theorem
A country exports the good that uses intensively its relatively abundant factor and imports the good that uses intensively its relatively scarce factor. Abundance can be defined in physical terms (K/L ratio) or price terms (relative factor prices); the price definition is the more robust. A capital-abundant country thus exports capital-intensive goods.
Factor price equalisation
Samuelson showed that, under the same assumptions, free trade equalises relative and absolute factor prices across countries: trade acts as a substitute for factor mobility. In practice, wages remain different because assumptions fail.
The Stolper–Samuelson theorem
An increase in the relative price of a good raises the real return to the factor used intensively in that good and lowers the real return to the other factor. Opening to trade raises the relative price of the exportable, so the abundant factor gains and the scarce factor loses. In a labour-abundant developing country, trade should raise real wages; in a capital-abundant country, it benefits capital owners at the expense of labour. This explains why labour unions in rich nations often favour protection.
Evaluation
The model is elegant and gives clear policy predictions, but Leontief's paradox, factor-intensity reversals, and technological differences complicate it. Yet the distributional insight remains central to modern debates on globalisation and wage inequality.
Conclusion
H-O explains the pattern of trade, S-S explains who wins and loses, and together they form the core of neoclassical trade theory.